# What is NOT sent to the daemon as build context.
#
# This file now DECIDES what ends up in the image, which it did not before. The
# Dockerfile used to copy five named things (package*.json, *.js, contexts,
# protos, env) and no rule here touched any of them; since the 2026-08-23
# reorganisation put every module in a subdirectory it copies the context whole,
# so that adding a protocol directory cannot be forgotten. Every exclusion below
# is therefore a decision about the image as well as about upload time — read the
# Dockerfile's COPY comment before adding one.
#
# node_modules is the whole reason this file exists. It is tens of megabytes, it
# is re-sent on every build, and it is usually PRESENT: `npm ci` here is the
# normal way to run the service outside a container, and the project this was
# extracted from installs these dependencies in its checkout of this repository
# as well — its compose files build with `context: .` pointed at that checkout,
# and four of its host-run tests load bbs2023.js in place, so they need the
# dependencies on disk. Without this file every one of those builds pays for it.
**/node_modules

# The checkout's own history. When this repository is consumed as a git
# submodule its .git is a small FILE rather than a directory; either way it is
# not something a build needs.
.git
.gitignore

# The runtime artefacts .gitignore already keeps out of commits: the signing keys
# are regenerated on every start by design, so a stray copy is stale as well as
# private.
*.pem
*.log

# The documentation and the CI definitions. Neither is read at runtime: docs/ is
# the source of the GitHub Pages site, .github/ is the workflows that build it
# and the container, and the CLAUDE.md files are guidance for whoever (or
# whatever) edits this repository. They are excluded because the Dockerfile now
# copies the context whole — before that they were left out by not being named.
docs
.github
CLAUDE.md
**/CLAUDE.md
README.md
COVERAGE.md
SECURITY.md
LICENSE.md
clean.sh
# The federation end-to-end test: three containers, a driver and a web
# application of its own. It BUILDS this image (its compose file's context is
# the repo root) and must not be INSIDE it — the harness is not part of the
# service, and the webapp has a separate build context of its own, so nothing
# here needs it. Excluding it also keeps a change to the test from busting the
# image layer cache.
federation-e2e
# The in-process contract tests, excluded for the same two reasons and a third.
# They assert this repository's MODULE CONTRACTS by requiring the modules
# directly, so they are a property of the source tree rather than of the running
# service: the image exists to run `server.js`, and nothing in it would ever
# call them. `npm test` is run from a checkout — which is where the modules
# being asserted are edited — and excluding them keeps an edit to a guard from
# busting the image layer cache. The `test` script in package.json therefore
# does not work inside the image, deliberately; package.json itself is copied
# for its dependency list.
tests
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
